r#  64626  1208  59
p: .
t. 
|  To examine further, imagine a simple programming language with
|  just a few components: function symbols, string (character-sequence)
|  and numeric literals, and <!!"!!>variable<!!"!!>
.  symbols that must be assigned a value before they are used


--- Sentence/switch
id: 362
r#  64627  1208  60


--- Sentence//end
id: 362
r#  64758  1210  38
p: .
t. 
|  The most common structure in such a language would be expressions consisting of a function
.  symbol and one or more <!!"!!>arguments<!!"!!>


--- Sentence/switch
id: 363
r#  64759  1210  39


--- Sentence//end
id: 363
r#  64905  1213  8
p: .
t. 
|   The interpreter
|  that would execute programs written in the language is essentially
|  a state machine, reading a sequence of symbols
.  in turn


--- Sentence/switch
id: 364
r#  64906  1213  9


--- Sentence//end
id: 364
r#  64949  1213  52
p: 
t. 
.   Assume the following basic operations:   


--- Sentence//end
id: 364
r#  65103  1218  43
g. 
|   @l(-%>) +64952/1213/55
|   +64956/1213/59:0=64956/1213/59
|   +64968/1213/71:0=64968/1213/71
.   @l() -64968/1213/71
p: .
t. 
|    If the current symbol is a numeric literal, convert it to an actual number
.  (e.g., <!!"!!>16<!!"!!> becomes hexadecimal )


--- Sentence/switch
id: 365
r#  65104  1218  44


--- Sentence//end
id: 365
r#  65198  1220  48
p: .
t. 
|   Convert
|  string literals to arrays of glyph
.  codes (e.g., in the Unicode  format)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 366
r#  65199  1220  49


--- Sentence//end
id: 366
r#  65244  1221  25
p: .
t. 
|   Place the current
.  value on a program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 367
r#  65250  1223  4


--- Sentence//end
id: 367
r#  65328  1224  30
p: .
t. 
|   If the current symbol is a variable, retrieve
.  its value from a global index


--- Sentence/switch
id: 368
r#  65329  1224  31


--- Sentence//end
id: 368
r#  65529  1227  77
p: .
t. 
|   Because every
|  variable is initialized before use, we can assume that
|  any value is ultimately obtained from source code
.  as in item , or read from an external source (like a local file)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 369
r#  65530  1227  78


--- Sentence//end
id: 369
r#  65580  1228  50
p: .
t. 
.  Again, place the current value on a program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 370
r#  65586  1230  4


--- Sentence//end
id: 370
r#  65680  1231  39
p: .
t. 
|   If the current symbol is a function-symbol, identify
.  which procedure is named by the symbol


--- Sentence/switch
id: 371
r#  65681  1231  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 371
r#  65886  1235  14
p: .
t. 
|   If we allow
|  polymorphism -=- one name may reference multiple code-blocks,
|  distinguished by their signatures -=- then this process
|  requires examining the types of values further down the
.  program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 372
r#  65887  1235  15


--- Sentence//end
id: 372
r#  66135  1239  42
p: .
t. 
|   Once the correct procedure is identified,
|  transfer control to that function body (if compiled
|  correctly, the new callee will take its inputs off the
|  stack and push its own return values, which in turn
.  may serve as inputs to later subroutines)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 373
r#  66136  1239  43


--- Sentence//end
id: 373
r#  66295  1244  9
p: .
t. 
|  
|  For sake of discussion, I will call this a <!!"!!>push/transfer<!!"!!> execution
|  model, given the two main operations (pushing a value, or transferring
.  control)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 374
r#  66296  1244  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 374
r#  66454  1246  48
g. 
|   @l(-%>>) +66369/1245/11
|   +66395/1245/37:20=66415/1246/9
.   @l() -66395/1245/37
p: .
t. 
|   While this account is too simplistic for practical computer
|  languages, it serves
.  as a useful case-study for real-world compilers

--- Sentence/end 
